Indigo's change: with DOLPHIN_DABR set (and the MMU emulated), the data
address breakpoint works as on a 750. libogc points the DABR at the lowest
doubleword of the running thread's stack and switches it with the thread, so
on a console a stack that overflows into its guard raises a DSI and libogc's
exception screen comes up; Dolphin ignored the DABR, and the overrun went on
corrupting memory. Every doubleword the DABR names gets a watch, which sends
the accesses there to the slow path, and MMU::Memcheck raises the DSI (DAR
the address, DSISR the DABR match and store bits), without making the access,
when it is the DABR's current one: a read if DR is set, a write if DW, while
MSR[DR] is as BT says. Each hit is logged ("DABR: write to ..."). GNU General
Public License version 2 or later, as Dolphin.

